  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 130 PM CDT Fri Aug 14 2026\n\nAny sort of interesting weather remains south this afternoon,\ntied to the surface boundary across Iowa, Illinois, and \nIndiana. We will start to see this pattern finally break down \nas a midlevel wave develops and spreads east from the Canadian \nRockies tonight. Spotty showers are expected tonight and \nSaturday as low level moisture increases in response to the \ndeveloping low pressure tied to the midlevel wave. Very little \nlightning is expected tonight given the lack of instability, \nthough increasing dewpoints tomorrow ahead of the cold front \nshould be enough to build in a thousand or so joules of MUCAPE. \nWhether or not we'll see any storms actually develop is \nuncertain, with only a few CAMs developing storms at all along \nthe front, and even less having them develop across Minnesota. \nThe bullish solution would be something like the 14.12Z run of \nthe RRFS. During this whole window (Friday afternoon - Saturday \nevening), we've kept PoPs capped at around 30 to 40% at any \ngiven time. In reality, the chance of precipitation is pretty \nhigh throughout the entire timeframe, but the confidence in the \nexact timing for any exact point is very low.\n\nHeading into Sunday, the front will have moved through and winds \nwill increase out of the northwest. Slightly cooler, and much \ndrier air will advect in. This will also help clear out any \nlingering wildfire smoke and cloud cover, making for the best \nday of the weekend.\n\nFor next week, Monday starts out similar to Sunday, with highs\nin the low 80s and dewpoints in the 50s and 60s. We will start\nto see a low level response to a deepening low pressure across \nCanada, increasing our winds out of the southwest. By Tuesday, \ndewpoints will increase well into the 60s and low 70s again, \nsetting the stage for our next chance for notable precip as a \nstrong cold front looks to sweep down from Canada. The latest \nNBM and WPC QPF amounts peak around 1-2\" across southern MN. \nThis is probably the area that needs it the least given the \ncurrent drought outlook, but it is good to see rain shifting \nnorth from the areas that have been slammed the past few days. \nShould our moisture flow get cutoff by any storms to our south, \nwe may see this number trend down. But as of now, this is our \nbest chance for heavy rain. CSU's MLP severe probability \nforecasts do also highlight low end chances for organized \nconvection both Monday and Tuesday.\n\nSurface high pressure should move in across the area again \nbehind the fropa, resulting in a couple of quieter days to end \nthe work week.",
